  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c4ef538c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The full regression output also shows that the treatment effect estimate, its standard error, and the p-value line up closely with the earlier hypothesis test results. In this example, regression is not changing the answer; it is simply expressing the same comparison in a more general statistical framework.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Problem with Peeking\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "- Your boss is impatient. Rather than waiting until all 1,000 visitors have been assigned to each group, she wants to run the hypothesis test after every 100 visitors per group — peeking at the results after 100, 200, 300, ..., up to 1,000 visitors per group (10 tests in total). If any of those tests comes back significant at the $\\alpha = 0.05$ level, she wants to declare a winner and stop the experiment early.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Explain to the reader why this is problematic under classical frequentist statistics. The key issue is that a single test at $\\alpha = 0.05$ has a 5% chance of a false positive (rejecting $H_0$ when it is actually true). But when you conduct multiple tests on accumulating data, each peek is another opportunity to get a false positive. The overall probability of at least one false rejection across all the peeks is much higher than 5%.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Demonstrate this with a simulation. The idea is as follows:\n",
    "\n",
    "    1. Set up a world where the null hypothesis is true: $\\pi_A = \\pi_B = 0.20$ (i.e., $\\theta = 0$, there is no difference between the CTAs).\n",
    "    2. Simulate a single experiment: draw 1,000 observations for group A and 1,000 for group B. After every 100 observations per group (at $n = 100, 200, \\ldots, 1000$), compute the z-statistic and p-value. Record whether any of the 10 p-values falls below 0.05.\n",
    "    3. Repeat step 2 a total of 10,000 times to simulate 10,000 experiments.\n",
    "    4. Compute the fraction of those 10,000 experiments in which at least one peek produced a significant result. This is the empirical false positive rate under peeking.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Compare this to the nominal 5% false positive rate of a single test. Report the result and explain to the reader why peeking inflates the Type I error rate and what this means in practice for running A/B tests.\n",
    ":::"
   ]
  }
 ],
